Specification AddressCorrection
Description Standardizes US street addresses through the Census Bureau geocoder

# Testing a live API. There are no production classes: the service is the thing
# under test, so the glue is all there is -- and there is very little of it.
#
# The service is the US Census Bureau's address geocoder, which is public and
# needs no API key:
#
#   https://geocoding.geo.census.gov/geocoder/locations/onelineaddress
#
# The attribute sets below mirror the shape of the JSON it answers with, so the
# generated Response.fromJSON reads the whole reply on its own. The glue never
# touches the JSON: it calls the service, hands the body to fromJSON, and
# compares. If glue is ever seen navigating a reply, the specification is not
# describing that reply honestly.
#
# Declaring a field means it must be present in the reply. Fields of the reply
# that are not declared are simply not read, which is why tigerLine and
# coordinates are absent here -- nothing is asserted about them.
#
# The service answers with the same data every time, so every expected value is
# a real one.

Background
Given base Page is : String
| https://geocoding.geo.census.gov |

# ============================================================
# A complete address comes back standardized
# ============================================================

Scenario Standardize an address that is already complete
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 1600 pennsylvania ave nw, washington, dc |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And the matched addresses are : Match
| matchedAddress                                  | addressComponents                          |
| 1600 PENNSYLVANIA AVE NW, WASHINGTON, DC, 20500 | 20500 PENNSYLVANIA WASHINGTON "" NW DC AVE |

# One row means one match. The count is the table, not a separate claim.
#
# addressComponents is a nested block, so its cell holds that block's text form:
# the values space separated, in the order the attributes are declared, with an
# empty one written "". The caller typed the address in lower case and left the
# ZIP off; case, and a ZIP that was never supplied, are what the service is for.

# ============================================================
# A spelled out street name becomes its numeral
# ============================================================

Scenario Correct a spelled out ordinal
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 350 fifth ave, new york, ny |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And the matched addresses are : Match CompareOnly
| matchedAddress                   |
| 350 5TH AVE, NEW YORK, NY, 10118 |

# "fifth" became "5TH" -- the reason anyone calls an address corrector.
#
# CompareOnly limits equality to the columns shown, so addressComponents is
# filled with the do-not-care marker all the way down and nothing inside it is
# compared. This table is horizontal because two candidates are checked below
# and several rows need a row each; a step may carry Vertical and CompareOnly
# together when one instance is being checked on its own.

# ============================================================
# Ambiguity returns every candidate
# ============================================================

Scenario Return every candidate for an ambiguous address
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 1600 pennsylvania ave, washington, dc |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And the matched addresses are : Match CompareOnly
| matchedAddress                                  |
| 1600 PENNSYLVANIA AVE SE, WASHINGTON, DC, 20003 |
| 1600 PENNSYLVANIA AVE NW, WASHINGTON, DC, 20500 |

# Drop the directional and the corrector cannot choose between the two
# Pennsylvania Avenues, so it returns both rather than guessing. Two rows means
# two matches: a third would fail on the count without anything being said
# about counting.

# ============================================================
# An address that does not exist
# ============================================================

Scenario Report an address that cannot be corrected
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 99999 nonexistent rd, nowhere, zz 00000 |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And there are no matched addresses

# A failure to correct is still a 200. The call succeeded; the address did not
# exist. Checking the status separately from the body is what keeps those apart
# -- a 404 here would be a defect in the service, not an unknown address.

Attributes Request
| Attribute | Type   | Default                            | Notes                                         |
| Method    | String | GET                                |                                               |
| Page      | String | geocoder/locations/onelineaddress  | Appended to the base Page from the Background |
| Address   | String |                                    | As the caller typed it                        |
| Benchmark | String | Public_AR_Current                  | Which edition of the address ranges           |
| Format    | String | json                               |                                               |

# Everything but Address has a default, so a scenario states only what is
# peculiar to it. The glue turns these into the query string.

Attributes Status
| Attribute | Type    | Default | Notes            |
| Code      | Integer | 200     | HTTP status code |

# ---- the shape of the reply -------------------------------------------------
# {"result": {"addressMatches": [ {"matchedAddress": ...,
#                                  "addressComponents": { ... }} ]}}

Attributes Response
| Attribute | Type   | Default | Notes                   |
| result    | Result |         | The one top level field |

Attributes Result
| Attribute      | Type            | Default | Notes                        |
| addressMatches | MatchCollection |         | Empty when nothing was found |

Collection MatchCollection
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                         |
| Match    | 0       | 10000   | One per address the API found |

Entity Match
| Attribute         | Type              | Default | Notes                          |
| matchedAddress    | String            |         | The standardized address       |
| addressComponents | AddressComponents |         | The same address, in its parts |

Entity AddressComponents
| Attribute       | Type   | Default | Notes                              |
| zip             | String |         | Five digits                        |
| streetName      | String |         |                                    |
| city            | String |         |                                    |
| preDirection    | String |         | N, S, E, W before the street name  |
| suffixDirection | String |         | NW, SE and so on, after the suffix |
| state           | String |         | Two letters                        |
| suffixType      | String |         | ST, AVE, RD, LN                    |

# The names are the JSON's own. The generator camelCases an attribute name to
# get the key it reads, and the Census keys are already camelCase, so these line
# up exactly. The five components not declared -- preType, fromAddress,
# toAddress, suffixQualifier, preQualifier -- are present in the reply and are
# simply not read.
